## Is IBD a disability?

Under the **Equality Act 2010**, a condition counts as a disability if it has a **substantial** (more than minor) and **long-term** (12 months or more) effect on your normal daily activities. The law looks at how you would be **without treatment**. Because IBD is lifelong and the law looks at you without treatment, most people with Crohn's or colitis are covered.

## Reasonable adjustments

- a desk close to a **toilet**, and toilet breaks when you need them
- flexible hours, or working from home during flares
- time off for appointments, infusions and surgery
- a phased return after time off

## Benefits

PIP is based on how IBD affects you on most days. It can affect activities such as:

- **managing toilet needs**, if you need help with incontinence or a stoma
- **managing treatments**, if you need help with injections or stoma care
- **planning and following journeys**, if fear of accidents causes overwhelming distress
- **preparing food**, and **washing**, if fatigue is severe

{% checker tool="pip" /%}

## If you cannot work

If your health means you cannot work, or can only work a little, you may be able to get [New Style Employment and Support Allowance](/benefits/esa/) or extra money in [Universal Credit](/benefits/universal-credit-health/). These are separate from PIP, and you can get them at the same time.

## Free prescriptions with a stoma

In England, you can get **free prescriptions** if you have a permanent stoma (such as a colostomy or ileostomy) that needs a bag or dressing. Ask for a **medical exemption certificate**. Prescriptions are free for everyone in Wales.

Free, independent advisers, such as **Citizens Advice** or a local welfare rights service, can help you check what you can claim and fill in forms.
